719|**Theorem 4.2 (finite versus loopy tempo). PROVED.**
720|- **(a)** For a short G, G ⊞ R_n^W behaves as "White has n one-shot passes". Its BIG translation G^B + n
721|  gives the right outcome when G ⊞ R_n^W is first-event dominant. A sufficient condition is pairwise safety
722|  in G together with B > n + max(M_L(G), M_R(G)).
723|  *Proof:* Theorem 1.11, with R_n counted as a component without threats; its n Left moves enter M_L.
724|- **(b)** If White's safe Z-moves form an acyclic graph and all Black Z-moves are suicides, then Z, with
725|  Black's suicides deleted (Lemma 1.7), equals the integer ℓ: the length of the longest path of safe White
726|  moves.
727|  *Proof:* induction, using {m₁, …, m_k | } = max m_i + 1. Dominated options can be deleted (KNOWN, [WW]
728|  ch. 2, [S] ch. II).
729|- **(c)** A reservoir with a White 2-cycle is on_D and equals no integer. *Proof:* take G = R_{n+1}^B, n + 1
730|  Black tempi with no threats.
731|  - G ⊞ R_n^W with White to move is L: the tempi run out on White first.
732|  - G ⊞ on_D with White to move is W: Black runs out.
733|
734|So a group's tempo is *finite* (a stopper equal to an integer) exactly when its spare moves are
735|non-reversible, and *unbounded* (on or off) when they contain a cycle.
736|
737|**Proposition 4.3 (no finite king tempo in isolated K+P groups). PROVED.** Let Z be a king group in a
738|position without officers, with a buffer. Suppose side s plays a non-capturing king move u → v inside F_Z,
739|and the opponent replies outside Z. Then v → u is legal at s's next turn and restores Z exactly.
740|
741|Consequently, if the opponent is frozen (all its Z-moves are suicides), any one safe king step of s gives s
742|an unlimited supply of passes. A king group's king tempo is therefore 0 (frozen) or ∞ (shuttle), never a
743|finite n ≥ 1.
744|
745|*Proof.*
746|- u is empty, since s's king just left it and the opponent did not move in Z.
747|- u is not adjacent to the opponent's king: that king has not moved since s's king stood on u legally.
748|- u is not attacked by an opponent Z-pawn, since none has moved. It is not attacked by a context pawn
749|  either: context pawns attack only context and buffer squares (Lemma 2.2), and u ∈ F_Z.
750|- With no sliders, the move exposes nothing. ∎
751|
752|**What this means for the "n safe spare moves" picture.** In king-and-pawn chess, a king with exactly n safe
753|spare moves cannot occur in an isolated group: one safe step and the step back already give infinitely
754|many.
755|- Finite tempo comes from **pawn** moves. That is Elkies' integers, spare pawn moves that end blocked
756|  (KNOWN, Elkies 1996).
757|- It can also come from genuine king-against-king fights, where the opponent *replies inside Z*: opposition
758|  and shouldering. Those are not reservoirs, and they can have any value.
759|
760|**Over.** over = {0 | over} (KNOWN, [WW] ch. 11, [S] ch. VI). Right can pass forever (over → over), and Left
761|has one move, to 0, that ends Right's passing.
762|
763|**Proposition 4.5. PROVED.** 0 < over < x for every number x > 0.
764|
765|*Proof.*
766|- **over > 0.** Left moving first plays to 0 and wins. Right moving first can only pass, and then Left plays
767|  to 0.
768|- **over − x < 0.** Right moving first passes. Left must then either play over → 0, leaving −x (negative, so
769|  Right wins), or move in −x, which Left can do only finitely often, Right passing in between. Either way
770|  Right wins. Left moving first faces the same two choices. ∎
771|
772|**Proposition 4.6 (over with decisive terminals). PROVED.** Let over_D have positions ω and 0_D, with
773|L(ω) = {0_D} and R(ω) = {ω}. If White wins G moving second, then White wins G ⊞ over_D with either side to
774|move.
775|
776|*Proof.* White's strategy:
777|- if White moves first, play ω → 0_D at once, leaving G with Black to move;
778|- if Black passes, answer ω → 0_D;
779|- if Black moves in G, answer with White's second-player strategy in G.
780|
781|If Black never passes, it runs out of moves in G and must then pass, which White answers by freezing. After
782|the freeze Black is to move in a G-position lost for Black. ∎
783|
784|**Chess reading of over.**
785|- Black has a safe king shuttle (endless passes), and White has exactly one move, typically a pawn move by
786|  Proposition 4.3, that takes away a shuttle square and leaves a mutual zugzwang.
787|- Elkies 1996 reports `over` in a study by Behting (1929), according to our literature notes. We have not
788|  re-checked it.
789|- **CONJECTURE 4.7:** no over-type group with ≤ 3 + 2 pawns fits on 3 files of the 8×6 cylinder, but over is
790|  realisable on the cylinder with wider groups. *Scratch evidence:* the first half; a geometric search found
791|  none. *To settle:* a wider search, then kpsolve.
