853|### 5.3 Position 7.3 (Kg8 Pf7 / Kf6 Pg7) — not a zugzwang at all
854|
855|- **Pattern C:** WK (X+1, H), WP (X, H−1), BK (X, H−2), BP (X+1, H−1), with X = f.
856|- **Both kings can shuttle.** Kg8–f8 and Kf6–g6 are safe (audit; PROVED by enumeration: every one of the
857|  four king placements is legal and keeps both pawns guarded).
858|- **But f7–f8=Q is available.** With the king on g8, the queen on f8 cannot be taken: the black king is two
859|  ranks away, or on e7 next to f8, which g8 guards.
860|- **Black cannot parry it. PROVED.** All 8 Black moves (Kf5, Kg5, Ke5, Ke6, Ke7, Kg6, g6, g5) leave f7–f8=Q
861|  available and safe.
862|- So Z₇.₃ is a White win **with either side to move**, in every non-terminal context, in P (P8):
863|  - the decisive event has T = 0 with White to move and T = 1 with Black to move;
864|  - Proposition 2.10(a) gives (L2), because Black cannot promote in one move from a non-terminal context;
865|  - (A_Q) is still needed for C.
866|- **Why the paper's "trébuchet = 0" is wrong here.** Z is not even a tempo game. It is also an instance of
867|  "a zugzwang where the other side's decisive event elsewhere is too slow": a Black passer created elsewhere
868|  is a ⊖ in the D-model but loses to f8=Q in P. So (V2) fails for Black's passers, and the D-model is wrong.
869|- **Without the pawn break.** If f8 were unavailable, the group would be a both-sides shuttle (dud-type), and
870|  Theorem 3.4(a) would make every threat-free sum a draw.
871|  - *Scratch evidence:* no both-sides-shuttle group without a pawn break exists among 3-file groups with
872|    ≤ 2 + 2 pawns and no passer on the 8×6 cylinder. dud may not occur in small K+P groups (CONJECTURE).
